<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tongwan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tongwancheng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tongwan was the capital of the Xia state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Helian Bobo</a> between 413 and 418 in the Ordos region, in today's Jingbian County in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. Its walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammed-earth">rammed earth</a> mixed with lime were famous for their hardness. Northern Wei captured it in 427, and its white ruins survive at the edge of the Mu Us Desert.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tongwan.jpg" alt="Tongwan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cong · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%BB%9F%E4%B8%87%E5%9F%8E.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tongwancheng, the city ruling ten thousand, stands at the southern edge of the Maowusu Sands of the Ordos Desert, on what was once a strategic site in the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordos-plateau">Ordos Plateau</a>, on the fertile upper reaches of the Wuding River. It is the largest urban centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> yet found. The city was built largely of wood, inside very thick outer walls coloured white with white clay and powdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, and had a lake at its centre. From a distance it was said to look like a giant ship, and it is also known as Bai Cheng, the White City.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was raised by around 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Helian Bobo</a>, also known as Liu Bobo, a descendant of the Xiongnu chanyu. He wanted it impenetrable and made his general Chigan Ali the architect. The soil of the walls was steamed to harden it, and if an iron wedge could be driven an inch into a section, the workmen responsible were executed. White cities were generally ceremonial and status centres built after conquest, white being a holy colour for the Xiongnu, but the walls were needed in a time of constant war. At its height the city had about 10,000 inhabitants.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Helian Bobo</a> died in 425 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-chang">Helian Chang</a>. In 426 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taiwu-of-northern-wei">Emperor Taiwu of Northern Wei</a> made a surprise attack: a brief incursion burned only the main temple, but the hinterland was devastated. The river and lake later dried up, possibly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a>, and the sands gradually buried the city. Tibetan forces besieged it in 786, and in 994 Emperor Taizong ordered it destroyed and its people moved, partly because the desert was spreading. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> soldiers invaded it in 1206, and by the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> it was abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins were rediscovered in 1845 and formally identified in 1956, and in 1996 they were listed as a Major Historical and Cultural Site Protected at the National Level. They lie in Jingbian County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, and are well preserved. The site was properly surveyed only in the 2000s and has not been fully excavated; its Yong'an Platform, where dignitaries inspected troops, has been restored.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tongwancheng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emperor Taiwu, enthroned in 423, attacked the Tiefu-led Helian Xia in 426 and took its capital <b>Tongwancheng</b> in 427; the Northern Liang in the Hexi Corridor submitted as a vassal, the Northern Yan fell in 436, and in 439 Taiwu conquered the Northern Liang outright, uniting the north…</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 413 and 418 he built the capital <b>Tongwan</b>, its walls of pounded earth mixed with lime so hard that, it was said, builders were executed if an awl could pierce them; its white ruins survive in northern Shaanxi.</blockquote><a href="/state/helian-xia">Helian Xia</a></li></ul></section>
